# gemma4-31b-dense-bf16-w4a8-df.recipe -- Gemma 4 31B dense (text) from Google's bf16 checkpoint
# at w4a8, as gemma4-31b-dense-bf16-w4a8.recipe makes it, with z-lab's DFlash drafter merged in
# place of the assistant head.
#
#   rad-convert <gemma-4-31B-it> --draft-model <gemma-4-31B-it-DFlash> \
#       --recipe data/recipes/gemma4-31b-dense-bf16-w4a8-df.recipe \
#       --set quantization_config.quant_method=compressed-tensors -o gemma4-31b-dense-w4a8-df.rad
#
#   radiance serve gemma4-31b-dense-w4a8-df.rad --num-speculative-tokens auto ...
#
# The drafter (z-lab/gemma-4-31B-it-DFlash) is five Qwen3-shaped layers of 5376 (64 heads / 8 kv
# x 128, ffn 10752) and an fc over the trunk's residual stream at layers 1 12 23 35 46 57: 1.5 B
# parameters, 3.1 GB at bf16. It denoises a block of 16 in one pass and proposes 15 tokens; z-lab
# measures 4.2-8.6 accepted per step on B300 at bf16, against the assistant head's ~2. The plugin's
# probe names that depth, so rad-convert declares at it and `--num-speculative-tokens auto` serves
# it; any other depth is refused by name.
#
# This starts from bf16, not from the QAT release. The drafter reads the trunk's hidden states at
# six layers and was trained beside the bf16 trunk, so the trunk that serves it should be as close
# to that one as four bits allow: rule=search, as in the plain bf16 recipe, which measured the
# assistant head accepting 0.71/0.62/0.63 of its k=3 drafts from this grid against 0.49/0.50/0.44
# from the QAT one.
#
# The trunk's lm_head is int8, as keptn42's container has it: logits_gemm_i8 reads it against the
# bf16 hidden state (no activation quantiser), with a bf16 scale per row and 128 columns. At tp 4 a
# rank's slice of the 262144 x 5376 table is 704 MB in bf16, a sixth of everything a verify step
# reads at int4, and 352 MB at int8. It is the trunk's verifier; drop the line to keep it bf16.
#
# ~20 GiB: the int4 trunk's linears (~14 GiB), the bf16 table and its int8 head (2 GiB), the
# vision tower, the drafter at block fp8 (1.4 GiB) and its head's block-fp8 copy (0.7 GiB).
#
# q, k and v are one linear, blk.*.attn_qkv (q | k | v; q | k on the global layers, where v is
# k): the plugin serves one GEMM a layer where three paid the skinny GEMM's fixed cost each. The
# grid's groups never cross a row, so the codes are those of three separate rules (on the cards k
# and v may round differently in the last bit: the GEMM's K split follows N). A container
# converted before this, with the three apart, still serves.

blk.*.attn_qkv.weight     rtn  codes=i4 group=128 scale=f16 rule=search
blk.*.attn_o.weight       rtn  codes=i4 group=128 scale=f16 rule=search
blk.*.ffn_down.weight     rtn  codes=i4 group=128 scale=f16 rule=search
blk.*.ffn_gate_up.weight  rtn  codes=i4 group=128 scale=f16 rule=search

output.weight             rtn  codes=i8 group=128 scale=bf16 clamp=sym

# The DFlash drafter's seven linears: block fp8, read at fp8a8 against an E4M3 activation (the
# drafter only proposes; the trunk verifies). All seven take one grid, because one quantised
# activation feeds several of them, and the plugin checks it.
dflash.fc.weight                 rtn codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.attn_q.weight       rtn codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.attn_k.weight       rtn codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.attn_v.weight       rtn codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.attn_output.weight  rtn codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.ffn_gate_up.weight  rtn codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
dflash.blk.*.ffn_down.weight     rtn codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16

# The drafter's head: the target's tied table again under a name of its own, block fp8, which the
# draft head reads instead of the bf16 table. That halves the widest read on the draft path (704 MB
# per rank at tp 4 in bf16), for half the table's bytes again. Drafts are verified, so this changes
# what is proposed, never what is accepted.
dflash.draft_head.weight         rtn codes=fp8_e4m3 block=128x128 scale=bf16
